<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=tram">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1807">1807</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Streetcar, Trolley, Tramway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A tram is an urban rail transit vehicle that runs on tracks on or beside public streets. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swansea">Swansea</a> and Mumbles Railway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, authorised by the Mumbles Railway Act of 1804, began horse-drawn passenger service in 1807, and it has been called the world's first passenger train or tram. Records show a street railway in Baltimore as early as 1828, and the first permanent tram line in continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1855. Fyodor Pirotsky tested the first electric tram line at Sestroretsk in 1875, and Frank J. Sprague built trams for the Richmond Union Passenger Railway in 1888.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tram.jpg" alt="Tram" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ŠJů (cs:ŠJů) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muzeum_MHD,_tramvaje_T3_8084_a_6149,_zepředu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1807</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Swansea, Wales</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Mumbles line was a one-off, and no street tramway appeared in Britain until 1860, when George Francis Train built one in Birkenhead. Records of a Baltimore street railway date from 1828.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/wheel">Wheel<span>before 4000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/domestication-of-the-horse">Domestication of the horse<span>c. 2200 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=tram">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
